What SHAP Does — In Simple Words
Think of your model like a doctor making a prediction: "This patient will deteriorate in the next 6 hours."

But right now, your model is like a black box — it gives a prediction but doesn't explain why. Your professor/evaluator will ask: "Why did the model say this patient is at risk?"

SHAP answers that question.

🔍 What SHAP Tells You
For each patient, SHAP breaks down the prediction and shows which features pushed the risk up or down:

Feature	Effect
Heart Rate = 145 (high)	⬆️ Pushed risk UP by +0.15
SpO2 = 88% (low)	⬆️ Pushed risk UP by +0.12
Blood Pressure = 120 (normal)	⬇️ Pushed risk DOWN by -0.05
On Vasopressors = Yes	⬆️ Pushed risk UP by +0.10
So now you can say: "The model predicted deterioration because the patient had a high heart rate, low oxygen, and was on vasopressors."

📊 The Two Main Plots You'll Get
SHAP Summary Plot (Global View)

Shows which features are most important overall across ALL patients
Example insight: "Heart rate and SpO2 are the top 2 most important features for predicting deterioration"
This goes in your report to prove your model learned meaningful medical patterns
SHAP Force Plot (Individual Patient View)

Shows one specific patient's prediction breakdown
Red arrows = features pushing toward deterioration
Blue arrows = features pushing toward stable
Great for your presentation/demo